 1 big thing: How BNY&#x27;s CEO uses AI tools 
 
 By Madison Mills 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo collage: Madison Mills/Axios. Photo: Courtesy of Jennifer S. Altman

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Robin Vince is CEO of America's oldest bank, which is also one of Wall Street's earliest AI adopters : It had over 100 "digital employees" in early 2025 before everyone else was using agents.

 Why it matters: This is our first installment in a series on how executives use AI tools. 

 What follows is an edited version of our conversation: 

 Axios: On a scale of 0 to 10, 0 being no AI usage and 10 being totally AI-pilled, where would you rank yourself? 

 Vince: "I'm probably a nine out of 10. I won't give myself a full score though, because one of the lessons for me in AI is that it's moving so quickly that actually being able to use it to its fullest extent feels like one of those quests you're never going to quite reach.

 "This feels to me like one of those lessons that is not unique to AI. We all have to be lifelong learners," Vince said, adding it's about "being OK with being a bit uncomfortable." Can you take me into an example of a recent instance where AI surprised you? 

 For a firm with 46,000 employees, "there's no person who can see the picture as completely, and so now to have AI be able to see a picture that you otherwise couldn't have seen and make recommendations in terms of what you'd actually do? That's a very significant thing." AI's "ability to see through all of these threads and hold them concurrently" to make suggestions is game-changing, Vince said. Take me into a day in your life of AI usage. 

 "This is where I humble myself because I'm sure that my usage of it is like 5% of what it could be if I was operating perfectly. ... [Eliza, BNY's in-house AI platform] understands my calendar. It understands my emails. It understands my documents, and so it coaches me at the beginning of the day, with how I should approach my day proactively." I have one minute left in our interview to try and break some news with you, since you were just added to OpenAI's board . Thoughts on IPO? Advice for them as a public company CEO? 

 "I've been on the board for all of two weeks, so I won't break any news. But for me ... being the chairman and the CEO of America's oldest bank, and then to see ... sort of the polar opposite of a very new company that's moving incredibly quickly at the intersection of one of the most important topics of our time ... what a privilege." Share this story. 

 
 
